From 0b41d84cf50e7eea178a1d0c6d56921dd7de42c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:28:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 323/324] gallium/pvr: cut the per-render cost of multi-render
 frames

Measured per render with the firmware trace and a per-flush log: terrain
generated its mip chains every frame and recreated a HWRT data set for
each of ~19 level sizes per render; desktop and shadow cleared the window,
rendered an offscreen pass, then came back to the window, which was a
full clear render plus a full load render (colour and ZLS depth).

- Small colour-only renders (<= 256x256, no depth) use the data set of
  their power-of-two size class (32..256); region, terminate and PBE clips
  keep them to their extent. Depth renders keep the exact size (the ZLS
  stores whole tiles).
- The mip generation background reads the source extent from a uniform,
  so one shader per format serves every level (was one per level size).
- Four render slots; a data set still takes at most two renders in
  flight (one per RT data, tracked per data set).
- A render that only clears is not submitted: the clear is recorded on
  the target (and depth buffer) and becomes the background of the next
  render into it. Sampling, CPU maps and blits of such a resource submit
  it first. Not for shared/scanout buffers.

A/B (alternating libgallium, perf governor, cores 6-7, 2 rounds):
desktop shadow 301/304 -> 398/397, shadow 184/181 -> 219/216,
terrain 11/11 -> 18/18, texture and conditionals unchanged.
gltri: all modes unchanged; new GLTRI_DEFER (clear, clear-only pass on
another FBO, draw without clear) and a cleared level 0 sampled
(GLTRI_MIPRT=0) match zink.
